ChatGPT said:
My conclusion

Yes—I understand the vision now, and it is much more coherent than the original “nuclear helicopter” problem.

You're essentially proposing a nuclear-electric ship with detachable airborne thrust capability:

Shipboard nuclear cell → electrical generation/distribution → electric aircraft → rotor thrust → force applied to the ship

The important conceptual shift is that the reactor never needs to fly. The ship carries the heavy, long-lived energy source and associated infrastructure. The aircraft only needs to carry the comparatively lightweight equipment necessary to convert supplied electrical energy into thrust.

That potentially addresses several of the problems we identified earlier:

    Nuclear fuel mass stays on the ship.
    Radiation shielding stays on the ship.
    Long-term reactor cooling stays on the ship.
    The aircraft doesn't need its own nuclear containment system.
    Electric motors are well suited to receiving power from an external electrical source.
    Aircraft can provide temporary thrust in directions that the ship's conventional propulsion system cannot easily provide, particularly during low-speed manoeuvring.

But there is one fundamental physics question

The aircraft cannot create a net force on the ship merely because it is electrically powered.

There has to be a physical force path between the aircraft and the ship.

For example, conceptually:

Ship → tether/connection → aircraft → air → aircraft thrust → tether → ship

or some other mechanism that transfers the aircraft's generated thrust to the ship.

If the aircraft simply flies alongside the ship without being mechanically connected, its thrust accelerates the air, not the ship in any useful sustained way.

So the heart of your idea isn't really the nuclear reactor.

It's the combination of:

    shipboard nuclear-electric power + airborne electric propulsion + a controlled method of transferring airborne thrust to the ship.

And that is a genuinely interesting engineering concept.

For docking specifically, the next question I'd investigate is whether an airborne thrust unit can provide useful manoeuvring force more efficiently and controllably than conventional thrusters, tugboats, azimuthing propellers, or bow/stern thrusters.

That's where your Vision becomes a real engineering comparison rather than just a nuclear-power thought experiment.

